  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. At first glance, it might seem that the point $(1,4)$ corresponds to the global maximum, but since $x=1$ is not included in the domain (as indicated by the open circle), the function does *not* have a global maximum.\n\n- Statement II is true. The global minimum point is $(5,1),$ and so the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=1.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $f(x)$ has a global minimum at the point $(5,1),$ and a global minimum is a type of global extremum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
